      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Published 2008 Wiley-Liss, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "A methacholine challenge may be used in confirming the diagnosis of asthma, occupational asthma, or reactive airways dysfunction syndrome (RADS) through identification of bronchial hyperreactivity (BHR). While sensitivity of the test in diagnosing clinically significant asthma is excellent, specificity of the test is poor. Since there are many conditions which have been associated with BHR, a positive test must be interpreted cautiously."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "This paper reviews potential causes of a positive methacholine challenge other than asthma or RADS which have been reported in the medical literature."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Factors which may be associated with a positive methacholine test include test methodology, normal variation of BHR in the general population, and numerous medical conditions."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "In cases of inhalation exposure evaluations, alternative explanations must be considered when determining whether a causal association exists between the exposure and a positive methacholine test result."
          }
        ]
      },
